From c1719014acac7c8149bc7e995dc4e200a5377559 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 19:41:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] gps: add network-initiated location and geofencing

Fetch IGnssNi and IGnssGeofencing alongside the assistance extensions and map
them onto the last nyx methods that were still unregistered: send_ni_response,
add_geofence_area, remove_geofence_area, pause_geofence and resume_geofence.

NI is the network asking the device for its position rather than the other way
round - emergency services and carrier SUPL requests. The HAL reports one
through niNotifyCb and the platform must notify, prompt, or comply according to
the NEED_NOTIFY / NEED_VERIFY / PRIVACY_OVERRIDE flags, then answer with
IGnssNi::respond. LuneOS can raise a popup, so the request can now actually
reach a person instead of timing out unanswered.

Geofencing hands a circle to the GNSS chip, which watches it autonomously and
reports entry and exit. The point is power: nothing has to hold a position
subscription open to notice a crossing. com.webos.service.location already
exposes /geofence/addGeofenceArea and /removeGeofenceArea over LS2 - LuneOS
patch 0005 re-enabled that category - so this one has a consumer already wired.

GnssNiNotification needed care rather than a plain struct read. Its two
hidl_string members are separate buffer objects in the parcel, so the pointers
inside the struct are meaningless here and the text is read from the parcel
afterwards, in field order. The strings are then copied into nyx's fixed inline
buffers, because the parcel is only valid for the duration of the callback. The
size assert caught the layout error this invites: declaring those members as
guint8[16] gives them alignment 1, which drops the padding a real hidl_string
forces and pulls every following field seven bytes forward. They are declared
as guint64 pairs so the compiler produces the 8-byte alignment, and the struct
is pinned at 64 bytes.

Geofence status codes needed no translation - nyx's NYX_GEOFENCER_* values are
the HIDL GeofenceStatus ones, including the negative errors - which is asserted
rather than assumed, as are the NI type, flag and response constants.

Both extensions follow the same optional-by-design rule as the assistance ones:
kept only if the interface and its setCallback both succeeded, with each method
answering NYX_ERROR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED otherwise.

While here, the five extension bring-ups had become five copies of one shape,
so they are now one helper driven by per-extension arguments, and the geofence
result callbacks - which are all (geofenceId, status) - share one decode.

Upstream-Status: Pending
